4 0 obj
<< /Length 917 >>
stream
BT /F2 10 Tf 50 750 Td (Syllora Academic Repository | Course: CS301 Operating Systems) Tj 400 0 Td (Page 2 of 3) Tj -400 -25 Td /F2 16 Tf (CS301: Tanenbaum - Modern Operating Systems Excerpt) Tj 0 -30 Td /F1 11 Tf 16 TL T* /F2 13 Tf (6.2 The Dining Philosophers Problem & Circular Wait) Tj /F1 11 Tf T* /F2 10 Tf (    "Deadlock requires circular wait across non-preemptible resources; breaking the cycle prevents deadlocks completely.") Tj /F1 11 Tf T* (Consider five philosophers sitting around a circular table with five chopsticks.) Tj T* (If every philosopher picks up their left chopstick at the exact same instant,) Tj T* (every philosopher will hold one chopstick and wait perpetually for the right one.) Tj T* (This textbook example illustrates circular wait in its purest form.) Tj T* (Starvation can also occur if two cooperative philosophers continually eat, leaving a middle philosopher starved.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
5 0 obj
<< /Length 744 >>
stream
BT /F2 10 Tf 50 750 Td (Syllora Academic Repository | Course: CS301 Operating Systems) Tj 400 0 Td (Page 3 of 3) Tj -400 -25 Td /F2 16 Tf (CS301: Tanenbaum - Modern Operating Systems Excerpt) Tj 0 -30 Td /F1 11 Tf 16 TL T* /F2 13 Tf (6.3 Deadlock Detection and Recovery) Tj /F1 11 Tf T* (When prevention is too restrictive, systems allow deadlocks to occur and periodically detect them.) Tj T* (Recovery strategies include:) Tj T* (1. Recovery through preemption: Temporarily taking a resource away from another process.) Tj T* (2. Recovery through rollback: Checkpointing processes periodically and restarting from a clean point.) Tj T* (3. Recovery through killing processes: Terminating one or more processes in the circular cycle.) Tj T* ET
endstream
